  "exact_replacement": {
    "change_id": "A2_LITERAL_RZ_PHASE_CONVENTION",
    "before": "An instruction on an old wire is lifted to the same numbered wire. This applies to bit flip, real y-rotation, z-rotation, and a controlled bit flip with distinct control and target. If an old target wire is removed from a word, equality of the remaining contexts on \\(n+1\\) wires means both equality of the old \\(n\\)-wire contexts and equality of the newly added highest bits. To prove the converse, examine a remaining wire: it is either the highest wire or an old remaining wire. This gives the two cases directly.",
    "after": "An instruction on an old wire is lifted to the same numbered wire. This applies to bit flip, real y-rotation, z-rotation, and a controlled bit flip with distinct control and target. If an old target wire is removed from a word, equality of the remaining contexts on \\(n+1\\) wires means both equality of the old \\(n\\)-wire contexts and equality of the newly added highest bits. To prove the converse, examine a remaining wire: it is either the highest wire or an old remaining wire. This gives the two cases directly.\n\nFor a real angle \\(\\theta\\), the z-rotation named here has the literal matrix\n\\[\n R_z(\\theta)=\\operatorname{diag}\\!\\left(\n \\cos(\\theta/2)-i\\sin(\\theta/2),\\;\n \\cos(\\theta/2)+i\\sin(\\theta/2)\\right).\n\\]\nOff-diagonal entries are zero. This is equality of complex matrix entries, not equality up to a global phase; in particular it is not silently replaced by \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\theta})\\). The instruction lift retains this same angle and matrix on the old target wire. The arbitrary one-qubit matrix lifting argument below therefore applies to this matrix as well. This convention adds no z-rotation to the later real RY/CX preparation tree.",
    "old_span": {
